const rousseauArticle: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'overview', title: 'Freedom inside dependence', paragraphs: [
    'Rousseau’s many subjects are connected by one persistent difficulty: human beings become dependent on one another without learning how to make that dependence compatible with freedom. Material interdependence is unavoidable in developed societies, but psychological dependence on rank, applause, and superiority can make each person live through other people’s judgments. Rousseau therefore asks how unequal social relations transform desire, how citizens could author legitimate law together, how education might form a comparatively independent person, and whether literary, musical, religious, and autobiographical expression can recover a voice not wholly governed by fashion. These are related investigations, not interchangeable versions of one doctrine.',
    'The works also resist a tidy system. The Discourse on Inequality reconstructs a conjectural social history; The Social Contract states principles of political right; Émile designs an artificial education for an imagined pupil; Julie tests love, family, authority, and community through fiction; the Confessions, Dialogues, and Reveries fashion different forms of self-account. Rousseau’s proposed male citizenship coexists with an education of Sophie toward dependence, and his celebration of domestic nurture sits beside his placement of his own children in a foundling institution. A comprehensive reading must preserve those collisions instead of converting him into either a democratic saint or a theorist of domination.',
  ]},
  {id: 'life-geneva-exile', title: 'Geneva, Paris, and the outsider’s posture', paragraphs: [
    'Born a citizen of Geneva in 1712, Rousseau lost his mother soon after birth and left the city as an adolescent. His migrations, religious conversions, employments, patronage relations, and irregular education placed him both inside and outside the institutions of the French Enlightenment. In Paris he worked on music, contributed to the Encyclopédie, and associated with Diderot and other philosophes. Yet he increasingly presented himself as their critic: intellectual sophistication, luxury, theatrical display, and elite sociability seemed to him capable of disguising dependence as refinement. The identity of solitary truth-teller was itself carefully made through publication, controversy, and retrospective narration.',
    'His career was materially precarious but never simply isolated. Patrons, copyists, publishers, friends, lovers, and correspondents sustained it. Thérèse Levasseur became his long-term partner and eventual wife. Rousseau later acknowledged that their five infants were sent to the Paris foundling hospital, an action that opponents used against the author of Émile. After the 1762 condemnations of Émile and The Social Contract, he moved through Swiss, Prussian, and British refuges before returning to France. Claims in the Confessions about revelations, motives, betrayals, and persecution remain indispensable evidence of self-understanding, but autobiographical narration cannot by itself settle disputed biographical facts.',
  ]},
  {id: 'first-discourse', title: 'The First Discourse: culture without virtue', paragraphs: [
    'The Discourse on the Sciences and Arts made Rousseau famous by answering that cultivated learning had not purified morals. His target was not knowledge as such. He attacked a social economy in which wit, taste, and accomplishment become badges of distinction, encouraging people to appear virtuous rather than become virtuous. Politeness can coordinate conduct while concealing rivalry; artistic brilliance can flourish in a corrupt polity; learned institutions can reward vanity and dependence on opinion. The polemical history is intentionally selective, so it should not be read as an empirical demonstration that every scientific advance makes societies worse.',
    'The essay introduces a method Rousseau would deepen: judge institutions not only by their declared purposes or magnificent products but by the kinds of motives and relations they cultivate. Its praise of austere civic virtue draws on ancient republican examples and provoked critics who saw hypocrisy in a writer using literature to condemn literary fame. Rousseau answered that diagnosis and remedy need not be identical: speaking within corruption might expose it, even if publication also entangles the critic in the desire for recognition. That unresolved problem of performing sincerity before an audience later becomes central to his autobiographical works and his conflict with the theater.',
  ]},
  {id: 'inequality-history', title: 'The Discourse on Inequality: a conjectural history', paragraphs: [
    'The Second Discourse does not report a discovered prehistoric society. Rousseau asks readers to set facts aside provisionally so that a conjectural sequence can distinguish what belongs to humans as such from what social history has made of them. The imagined solitary human has simple needs, self-preserving amour de soi, pity toward suffering, a capacity for free response, and perfectibility—the open-ended ability to change. This figure is neither morally virtuous nor vicious in a developed sense because durable comparison, obligation, language, and social identity have not yet formed. “Natural goodness” therefore cannot mean possession of a complete moral code before society.',
    'Contingent developments—tools, cooperation, family association, settled comparison, agriculture, metallurgy, property, and political institutions—produce both capacities and injuries. Rousseau’s intermediate scenes can make early sociality look comparatively happy, but the sequence is not a simple fall from a fully self-sufficient paradise. Perfectibility makes learning, affection, language, reason, and morality possible while also enabling domination. The narrative exposes how institutions come to appear natural after history has sedimented them. Its speculative anthropology and broad claims about peoples and climates should be studied as eighteenth-century philosophical construction, not treated as current archaeology or ethnography.',
  ]},
  {id: 'amour-propre', title: 'Amour-propre, comparison, and recognition', paragraphs: [
    'Rousseau distinguishes basic self-concern, amour de soi, from amour-propre, the socially formed concern with one’s standing in other people’s eyes. The distinction is not merely between good self-love and bad selfishness. Amour-propre requires comparison: one wants recognition and often wants to be preferred. It can drive vanity, humiliation, envy, resentment, and the wish to dominate, especially where ranks are unstable enough to invite competition but unequal enough to make recognition scarce. People then need others not only for goods and labor but for a sense of worth, allowing opinion to govern the self from within.',
    'Yet a human life with language, morality, and citizenship cannot simply delete the comparative passion. Rousseau’s political and educational projects try to redirect it. Equal citizenship may give each person a standing that does not depend on personal subordination, while a carefully staged education can postpone competitive vanity and teach esteem for genuine capacities rather than fashionable applause. Scholars disagree over how thoroughly amour-propre can be made benign, and Rousseau often sounds more pessimistic than programs of rehabilitation suggest. His lasting insight is that dependence is psychological and institutional at once: formal freedom may coexist with lives organized around status anxiety and unequal recognition.',
  ]},
  {id: 'property-inequality', title: 'Property, domination, and political economy', paragraphs: [
    'The Discourse’s famous scene of the first claimant enclosing land condenses a long transformation rather than naming one datable founder of property. Stable possession becomes socially consequential when agriculture, division of labor, accumulated goods, and public recognition make some people dependent on others’ resources. The rich then propose political association under the language of security and justice, but the resulting compact can convert advantageous possession into law and institutionalize inequality. Rousseau is analyzing how power gains legitimacy by presenting a partial interest as common protection, not supplying a single causal history of every state.',
    'The later Discourse on Political Economy and Social Contract do not simply abolish possessions. They ask how property can exist under public law without enabling one citizen to buy another and another to be forced to sell himself. Political economy therefore concerns civic formation and distribution as well as administration. Rousseau does not provide a modern program of income equality, and his constitutional writings accept historically specific forms of property. Still, the demand that no citizen be rich enough to command another or poor enough to become dependent marks material inequality as a threat to political freedom, not merely a private difference in holdings.',
  ]},
  {id: 'freedom-forms', title: 'Natural, civil, and moral freedom', paragraphs: [
    'Freedom changes meaning across Rousseau’s works. The natural human enjoys independence from another person’s will and a capacity not to follow instinct mechanically. Social development expands powers and attachments but also creates subjection. The Social Contract distinguishes the unregulated liberty of the prepolitical condition from civil liberty under laws and from moral freedom, in which a person acts through a will shaped by reasons and commitments rather than appetite alone. The point is not that any restraint improves freedom. Law is liberating only when it belongs to a legitimate order in which citizens participate as equals in making the general terms that bind them.',
    'This account makes non-domination and authorship more central than an unlimited range of choices. A person can have many consumer options while remaining dependent on a master, patron, creditor, or public opinion. Conversely, accepting a genuinely general law can express rather than cancel agency. The argument is powerful and hazardous because actual citizens may disagree over whether a law is general or whether political formation has manipulated their will. Rousseau’s formula about coercing a recalcitrant citizen into freedom names this problem rather than solving it: enforcement might sustain a self-given common law, or it might let rulers redescribe domination as the citizen’s true will.',
  ]},
  {id: 'social-contract', title: 'The Social Contract: a standard of legitimacy', paragraphs: [
    'The Social Contract begins from existing subjection and asks what could make political authority right. Force, conquest, slavery, paternal analogy, and a ruler’s supposed natural superiority cannot generate obligation. A legitimate pact must associate persons so that each gives himself under the same terms as every other, producing a public person whose members are citizens as participants in sovereign authority and subjects as bearers of law. The argument is normative and institutional: it does not claim that modern states literally began in one meeting or that tacit residence automatically repairs conquest and exclusion.',
    'Rousseau calls every legitimate state a republic because law, not the private will of a ruler, governs it. This does not mean every legitimate government is a direct democracy in the ordinary modern sense. The sovereign is the citizen body acting legislatively, whereas government is the commissioned executive that administers law. Democratic, aristocratic, or monarchical forms can describe the executive, and Rousseau often doubts whether popular administration can work. Confusing sovereign authorship with governmental form turns his analysis into a slogan and hides his central concern: how officials can remain agents of a people whose legislative authority cannot be alienated to them.',
  ]},
  {id: 'general-will', title: 'General will and common interest', paragraphs: [
    'The general will is not whatever a majority happens to desire, the sum of private preferences, or a mystical mind floating above citizens. It is the orientation of citizens toward the common good as they legislate general rules for a body of equals. Votes may disclose that orientation only under demanding conditions: citizens must be adequately informed, avoid organized factions that substitute group interests for the whole, and confront questions framed as general laws rather than favors to persons. Even then, Rousseau’s language permits competing interpretations about whether the general will is primarily a procedure, a common interest, or a civic disposition.',
    'The difference between the general will and the will of all explains both the doctrine’s promise and its danger. People can collectively choose privileges, scapegoats, or policies that burden some while exempting others; numerical agreement does not make those choices general. But no external expert or ruler receives a license to announce what the people really will independently of their political agency. The legislator may help a founding people see institutional possibilities, yet cannot possess sovereign authority. Rousseau leaves difficult questions about persistent minorities, plural interests, large states, deliberation, and bad civic formation. Later democratic theories develop different answers rather than simply applying one complete model.',
  ]},
  {id: 'sovereignty-government', title: 'Sovereignty, representation, and institutions', paragraphs: [
    'Sovereignty is inalienable and indivisible because a people cannot transfer the act of willing general law without ceasing to be politically self-governing. Deputies may administer, advise, or carry mandates, but representatives cannot replace the sovereign citizen body. Rousseau’s attack on English electoral complacency therefore concerns the periodic surrender of legislative agency, not a claim that every public task must be performed by all citizens. His small-republic assumptions and suspicion of representation create obvious difficulties for populous modern states, while his later projects for Corsica and Poland show greater attention to federation, local assemblies, offices, and historically available institutions.',
    'Government is always liable to acquire a corporate interest and usurp the sovereign. Rousseau accordingly examines assemblies, magistracies, tribunes, dictatorships, censorship, voting rules, and periodic tests of whether citizens wish to maintain the political form. These mechanisms do not amount to the familiar textbook division of powers, nor do they guarantee liberty. Civic mores and relative equality must support them. The analysis combines hard institutional realism with a demanding ideal of popular authorship: constitutions decay when citizens become passive clients, but intense unity can also suppress difference. Moderating executive power and sustaining active sovereignty remain problems, not achievements guaranteed by correct terminology.',
  ]},
  {id: 'civil-religion', title: 'Religion, conscience, and civic unity', paragraphs: [
    'Rousseau’s religious writings cannot be reduced to either orthodox Christianity or secular republicanism. The Profession of Faith of the Savoyard Vicar in Émile offers a staged first-person argument for conscience, providence, freedom, and a simple natural religion while criticizing dogmatic systems and clerical authority. The speaker is a character, and the profession’s relation to Rousseau’s final beliefs is debated. Its appeal to inward sentiment does not dispense with reasoning; it challenges philosophical proofs and institutional claims that outrun the capacities and moral needs of ordinary persons.',
    'The Social Contract’s civil religion addresses a different problem: what minimal commitments can sustain civic obligation when churches claim independent authority. Rousseau rejects a national priesthood that fuses theological rule with sovereignty, yet his proposed civic profession includes belief in providence, afterlife, and the sanctity of the social bond, alongside rejection of intolerance. Most troublingly, someone who publicly accepts the civic dogmas but behaves as though they mean nothing may face severe punishment. The chapter tries to prevent sectarian domination, but its coercive boundary shows the cost of demanding unity. Toleration and exclusion remain in unresolved tension.',
  ]},
  {id: 'emile-education', title: 'Émile: education through arranged independence', paragraphs: [
    'Émile is a philosophical novel and thought experiment about forming a man who can live in society without being wholly ruled by it. Its tutor does not merely let nature unfold. He manipulates environments, times lessons, controls encounters, and lets consequences appear to come from things rather than commands. “Negative education” means delaying premature moralization and bookish instruction while protecting the child’s developing powers. The pupil learns through bodily activity, craft, necessity, and carefully designed experience. This is discipline made indirect, not an absence of discipline, and it depends on resources and authority unavailable to most actual children.',
    'The sequence follows changing needs from infancy through adolescence, moral awakening, religious questioning, sexuality, travel, and preparation for social life. Rousseau opposes teaching words before the learner can connect them to judgment, yet his tutor’s near-total control raises the problem of whether autonomy can be manufactured through concealed dependence. Émile’s manual craft and encounters with labor aim to puncture inherited status, while his eventual wealth and male mobility remain assumed. The book influenced child-centered education, but it is not a ready curriculum: it combines developmental insight, social criticism, literary staging, and an authoritarian pedagogy that readers must assess together.',
  ]},
  {id: 'emile-citizenship', title: 'Émile between person and citizen', paragraphs: [
    'Émile distinguishes education for a human being from education for a citizen. Ancient civic orders could form persons wholly for a political whole, Rousseau suggests, but modern societies lack the unity required for that model. The tutor therefore cultivates a person able to preserve judgment amid corrupt institutions. Yet withdrawal cannot solve dependence: Émile must learn work, property, compassion, sexuality, religion, travel, and political obligation. The book’s relation to The Social Contract is thus neither identity nor opposition. One asks how a political people could be free; the other asks how an individual might remain comparatively whole when such a people scarcely exists.',
    'The educational project also tests the difference between natural development and social role. Many lessons answer genuine changes in capacity, but the final formation channels Émile toward marriage, household authority, and a specifically male form of independence. His tutor prepares him to judge laws while Sophie is prepared to influence a husband. Rousseau sometimes presents this asymmetry as natural complementarity, but the educational machinery used to produce it reveals extensive convention. Mary Wollstonecraft would exploit precisely that fault line: if dependence and flattery deform persons, educating women for dependence cannot be justified by pointing to the traits that dependent education has created.',
  ]},
  {id: 'gender-sophie', title: 'Sophie, gender, and the boundary of equality', paragraphs: [
    'Book V of Émile assigns Sophie an education oriented toward pleasing, regulating, and supporting a husband. Rousseau grants women reason and moral agency, but directs their cultivation toward domestic influence rather than equal public citizenship. Modesty, reputation, religion, and indirect power become central because women are imagined as sexually and socially dependent. This is not a passing prejudice detachable from the rest of the work: gendered households help reproduce the civic order, and the male citizen’s independence is partly supported by female service. The text’s universal language of freedom therefore encounters a deliberately sexed institutional boundary.',
    'Rousseau’s fiction complicates but does not erase that boundary. Julie exercises moral authority, organizes Clarens, and shapes a community, yet her agency works through marriage, domestic management, sacrifice, and feeling. Readers disagree about whether these figures reveal spaces of female value inside Rousseau’s framework or expose the instability of his claims about nature. A historically responsible critique need not pretend he offered contemporary gender equality. It should show why Wollstonecraft and later feminist readers found his own analyses of dependence, artificial desire, and corrupted appearance usable against the social arrangements he prescribed for women.',
  ]},
  {id: 'julie', title: 'Julie: desire, household, and moral authority', paragraphs: [
    'Julie, or the New Heloise was an eighteenth-century literary sensation, not a detachable illustration of the political treatises. Through exchanged letters it stages love across rank, the force of parental authority, the demands of virtue, religious difference, friendship, marriage, and the management of a rural household. Julie’s relation with Saint-Preux cannot be fulfilled without colliding with family and status. Her later life at Clarens seeks to reorganize passion through domestic order and benevolent authority. The novel makes readers feel the attraction and cost of arrangements that abstract doctrine might describe too quickly.',
    'Clarens is neither simply Rousseau’s democratic republic nor an innocent private refuge. Its harmony depends on property, paternal household rule, gendered labor, surveillance, and the moral prestige of Julie and Wolmar. At the same time, its practices of work, festivity, mutual attention, and limited luxury explore how dependence might become less humiliating. The epistolary form prevents one narrator from closing every dispute: sentiment, confession, observation, and self-deception circulate among voices. Julie belongs beside the political and educational works because it examines authority through intimate attachments, but its tragic plot and literary polyphony should not be flattened into a constitutional blueprint.',
  ]},
  {id: 'nature-solitude', title: 'Nature, solitude, and attention', paragraphs: [
    '“Nature” changes function across Rousseau’s writing. It can mean original dispositions before extensive social comparison, a developmental order the tutor should respect, the nonurban world of walking and botany, or a normative contrast used to expose artificial needs. It does not mean that every spontaneous impulse is good or that civilization can be reversed. The solitary human of the Second Discourse, the managed childhood of Émile, and the reflective walker of the Reveries occupy different literary and philosophical situations. Treating them as one call to primitivism loses the work Rousseau asks nature to do.',
    'In the Reveries, walking, plant study, memory, and absorbed sensation offer intervals in which a persecuted self can loosen dependence on public judgment. Solitude is restorative but ambiguous. Rousseau seeks truthful self-presence while continuing to address imagined readers and narrate injuries inflicted by others. Botanical attention can interrupt social drama, yet the autobiographical voice repeatedly returns to reputation. The late writing does not prove that freedom requires permanent isolation. It experiments with limited practices of attention when civic reconciliation and social trust seem unavailable, thereby exposing both the need for relations and the desire to escape their power.',
  ]},
  {id: 'music-language-theater', title: 'Music, language, theater, and representation', paragraphs: [
    'Rousseau was a composer, music theorist, copyist, and controversial participant in debates over French and Italian opera. He favored melody and expressive accent over theories that made harmony and mathematical relation fundamental. His Essay on the Origin of Languages connects early speech with passion, gesture, song, climate, and social gathering, making communication an embodied history rather than the transmission of neutral propositions. These arguments contain broad geographical contrasts that belong to eighteenth-century conjecture, but they illuminate why voice matters politically: expression can reveal presence, while elaborate systems of representation may distance speakers from what they feel.',
    'The Letter to d’Alembert on the Theatre brings aesthetics into civic analysis. Rousseau denies that establishing a theater in Geneva would automatically improve morals; spectatorship, professional performance, luxury, and imitation may weaken active civic attachments. He contrasts theater with participatory public festivals, though the contrast idealizes communal unity and polices gendered behavior. His own dramatic and operatic activity makes the position complicated rather than merely hypocritical. Music and theater show the same question found in politics and autobiography: can representation transmit a shared world, or does it substitute appearance and dependence on performers for direct participation?',
  ]},
  {id: 'autobiography', title: 'Confessions, Dialogues, and Reveries', paragraphs: [
    'The Confessions promises an unprecedented presentation of an individual, but its importance lies in the constructed practice of self-disclosure, not guaranteed transparency. Rousseau arranges memory into scenes of shame, pleasure, injury, desire, authorship, and moral explanation. He admits actions that damage his authority while framing their motives and their place in a singular life. This makes the work a major experiment in modern autobiography. It also requires critical distance: remembered dialogue, causal interpretation, and assertions about enemies are narrative acts, and the reader cannot infer that courageous confession makes every recollection independently verified.',
    'The later Dialogues and Reveries change the tribunal. In the Dialogues, “Rousseau” judges “Jean-Jacques” against a hostile public image, dividing the self to answer persecution. The Reveries turns toward walking, recollection, conscience, happiness, and the limits of communication. Across these texts autobiography becomes a philosophical laboratory for sincerity: how can a person know and show himself when social opinion distorts both speaker and audience? The answer never stabilizes. Writing seeks independence from judgment while demanding a fair reader. That contradiction links self-narration to amour-propre and makes the autobiographical corpus integral to Rousseau’s philosophy rather than incidental gossip.',
  ]},
  {id: 'contradictions', title: 'Contradictions that interpretation must preserve', paragraphs: [
    'Rousseau’s contradictions are not all of one kind. Some are differences among genres and questions: a conjectural history need not prescribe a constitution, and a fictional tutor need not describe ordinary schooling. Others are substantive tensions: equal political authorship is restricted to men; civic unity pressures religious dissent; education for autonomy uses hidden control; hostility to psychological dependence coexists with an intense struggle over public reputation. Biography sharpens further conflicts, especially between educational authority and abandonment of children. Explaining context can prevent easy anachronism, but it cannot dissolve the ethical force of these collisions.',
    'Nor does inconsistency make the corpus worthless. Rousseau supplies unusually powerful tools for asking how domination becomes intimate, how inequality shapes desire, and how institutions might let persons obey laws without serving another person’s will. Those tools also reveal limits in his own prescriptions. Reading him critically means moving between reconstruction and immanent challenge: identify the precise project of each work, then ask where its categories exclude persons or where proposed remedies reproduce diagnosed harms. A unified system obtained by erasing differences would be less faithful than a map of recurring problems, attempted solutions, failures, and revisions.',
  ]},
  {id: 'reception', title: 'Revolution, Romanticism, democracy, and authoritarian readings', paragraphs: [
    'Rousseau influenced Kant’s moral and political thought, revolutionary languages of popular sovereignty, republican and democratic theory, educational reform, literature, autobiography, music, and Romantic attention to feeling and nature. These receptions selected and transformed different parts of the corpus. French revolutionaries could invoke general will or civic virtue without reproducing Rousseau’s institutional qualifications; later democrats could develop popular authorship through representative or deliberative forms he distrusted. Romantic writers inherited nature, inwardness, and expressive authenticity, while critics of modern society adapted his analyses of comparison and alienation to circumstances he never knew.',
    'Hostile traditions have made Rousseau an ancestor of totalitarianism, often emphasizing civic unity and coercion in the name of freedom. Defenders have made him an uncomplicated champion of democracy. Neither lineage is a neutral report of what one text commands. The Social Contract contains real openings for coercive appropriation as well as restrictions on rulers claiming to embody sovereignty; the wider corpus contains egalitarian diagnoses and gendered exclusions. Reception should therefore be traced as argument and use, not direct inheritance. Rousseau matters partly because opposed traditions can find resources in him, and because the disputes force readers to specify what institutions make collective self-rule non-dominating.',
  ]},
  {id: 'reading-path', title: 'A reading path through distinct works', paragraphs: [
    'Begin with the Discourse on Inequality, keeping its conjectural method and sequence of amour de soi, pity, perfectibility, comparison, property, and political legitimation in view. Then read The Social Contract as a separate inquiry into right, marking the distinctions among sovereign, government, law, and decree. Émile should follow, especially Books II, IV, and V, because it tests how capacities and dependence are formed before political participation. Read the Profession of Faith as speech by a character and the education of Sophie as a central political problem, not an expendable appendix.',
    'Next read substantial selections from Julie so that household authority, rank, desire, and sentiment appear in a genuinely literary setting. The Letter to d’Alembert and writings on language or music reveal how representation and civic presence connect. Finish with the Confessions and Reveries, comparing the tribunal of public opinion with the search for solitary attention. Specialist companions are most useful when they keep interpretive disagreement visible. No slogan—natural goodness, the general will, child freedom, authenticity—should replace this sequence of different works, and no single verdict should pre-empt the contradictions the sequence makes legible.',
  ]},
];

const montesquieuArticle: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'overview', title: 'Laws in relation', paragraphs: [
    'Montesquieu’s central achievement is a comparative way of thinking about law. Rules cannot be understood only as commands or measured against one abstract constitution. They stand in relations to a form of government, its animating passions, territory, economy, religion, customs, history, climate, and neighboring powers. The Spirit of the Laws tries to make the apparent variety of institutions intelligible without assuming that one arrangement fits every people. That ambition makes Montesquieu a major analyst of political conditions, but it also exposes his work to criticism when explanation slips into stereotype or inherited reports substitute for adequate knowledge.',
    'The familiar three-branch formula captures only one part of this project. Montesquieu studies why legal security depends on distributed power, how criminal procedure shapes liberty, how commerce alters manners and international relations, how empires expand and decay, and how slavery is condemned yet partially rationalized through climate. He is a thinker of moderation because political goods are fragile and powers need checks, not because every compromise is just. Persian Letters, Considerations on the Romans, and The Spirit of the Laws use satire, historical narrative, and systematic comparison to reveal different dimensions of domination.',
  ]},
  {id: 'life-office-travel', title: 'Magistrate, traveler, and author', paragraphs: [
    'Charles-Louis de Secondat was born at La Brède near Bordeaux in 1689 into a noble family. Trained in law, he inherited the title Montesquieu and a senior judicial office in the Parlement of Bordeaux, an institution with judicial and administrative functions rather than a modern legislature. His work in criminal justice, participation in the Bordeaux academy, estate management, and engagement with natural inquiry informed his interest in how rules operate within institutions. Persian Letters appeared anonymously in 1721 and established his literary reputation before he resigned his office and traveled through Europe.',
    'Montesquieu spent roughly two years in England and observed political practice, but The Spirit of the Laws is not a transcript of an English constitution. He filtered travel, reading, classical history, legal knowledge, and reports about non-European societies through a comparative argument developed over many years. Considerations on the Causes of the Greatness of the Romans and their Decline appeared in 1734; The Spirit of the Laws followed in 1748 and drew both acclaim and religious condemnation. His failing eyesight shaped later work, and he died in 1755 after defending the book against critics.',
  ]},
  {id: 'persian-letters', title: 'Persian Letters and the foreign gaze', paragraphs: [
    'Persian Letters uses fictional travelers, Usbek and Rica, to make French institutions look strange. The epistolary form shifts perspective rather than installing one reliable spokesperson: jokes about Parisian fashion, monarchy, papal authority, learned culture, and demographic decline coexist with reflections on law, toleration, and government. Imagined Persian observers expose European vanity, but their observations also rely on an Orientalist literary device. The work should be read as satire within a history of European representations of Persia, not as documentary testimony about Iranian society.',
    'The letters from Usbek’s seraglio turn the observer into an object of judgment. He can criticize public despotism while ruling wives and eunuchs through distance, surveillance, mutilation, and fear. Roxana’s final resistance exposes the contradiction between his enlightened maxims and domestic mastery. This plot makes gendered and enslaved dependency central to Montesquieu’s early analysis: domination damages rulers’ self-knowledge as well as subjects’ lives. It also warns against identifying the author with any single letter writer. Philosophical insight emerges through irony, sequence, competing voices, and the collapse of Usbek’s authority.',
  ]},
  {id: 'comparative-method', title: 'Comparison, causes, and the spirit of laws', paragraphs: [
    'In The Spirit of the Laws, “spirit” names a network of relations rather than a mystical national essence. Laws relate to political structure, motive, population, geography, subsistence, wealth, commerce, religion, manners, and one another. Comparison asks why an institution operates differently when these relations change. Montesquieu therefore resists both arbitrary cataloging and a universal legislative recipe. Explanation has a practical purpose: reformers who isolate one law may destroy supports they did not see, while those who understand relations can identify where humane change is possible without turning caution into reverence for every inherited abuse.',
    'This method mixes general types with historical cases and evidence of uneven quality. Montesquieu’s reports about Asia, Africa, the Americas, and ancient societies often depend on travelers, classical authors, and European commonplaces. His causal language sometimes treats physical and moral causes as interacting, yet it can still harden broad populations into stereotypes. The work helped form comparative political and social inquiry, but priority claims should be restrained and its evidence criticized. Its most durable lesson is methodological modesty: institutions are relational and path-dependent, so explanation and judgment require more than exporting a favored constitutional diagram.',
  ]},
  {id: 'government-principles', title: 'Forms, principles, and corruption', paragraphs: [
    'Montesquieu distinguishes republics, monarchies, and despotisms by their nature—who rules and through what structure—and by their principle, the passion that keeps the form moving. Democratic republics need political virtue understood as love of laws and country; aristocracies need moderation among nobles; monarchies use honor and ranked intermediary bodies; despotisms operate through fear. These are analytical types, not claims that every actual regime fits perfectly. A monarchy is distinguished from despotism by fixed law and intermediate powers, not merely by having one ruler.',
    'Corruption occurs when a regime’s structure or sustaining motive is undermined. Democratic equality can decay through oligarchic inequality, but Montesquieu also fears an “extreme equality” that rejects every delegated office. Monarchy becomes despotic when a ruler destroys intermediary bodies and law-governed distinctions. Despotism is already corrupt in principle because fear suppresses deliberation and leaves ruler and subjects insecure. The analysis makes political psychology institutional: laws educate motives, and motives sustain laws. It can explain stability without endorsing hierarchy, though Montesquieu sometimes treats inherited noble bodies more favorably than modern egalitarian readers will accept.',
  ]},
  {id: 'liberty-powers', title: 'Liberty, security, and distributed power', paragraphs: [
    'Political liberty for Montesquieu is not doing whatever one wants. It is the security, or experienced confidence of security, that comes when law limits arbitrary force. Because holders of power tend to extend it, power must check power. His account of the English constitution distributes legislative, executive, and judicial functions and gives institutions means to resist encroachment. The aim is motion without domination: bodies should be able to act, but not to combine public powers so completely that subjects must fear an uncontrolled will.',
    'This is more complex than assigning three sealed branches to separate buildings. Montesquieu discusses bicameral legislation, executive veto, taxation, temporary judging, social orders, and relations among powers. His description idealizes parts of eighteenth-century England and belongs to a monarchical context. Later constitutional framers transformed it. The broader principle is that liberty depends on legal forms, counterweights, and social conditions that prevent concentration. A copied tripartite chart can coexist with arbitrary administration, while institutions not matching the chart exactly may still distribute authority. The test is whether citizens are secure from uncontrolled power.',
  ]},
  {id: 'criminal-law', title: 'Moderation in criminal law', paragraphs: [
    'Montesquieu connects constitutional liberty to ordinary criminal procedure. Vague offenses, punishment of thoughts, charges difficult to prove, unnecessary penalties, torture, and unchecked accusation make citizens insecure even when institutional branches look balanced. Law should address conduct that threatens public order, define offenses clearly, and make punishment proportionate to necessity. He is especially alert to religious crimes and accusations such as witchcraft or sexual offenses that can open paths for calumny. This attention influenced later penal reform and shows why liberty cannot be reduced to elections or the formal location of powers.',
    'Moderation is therefore an architecture of restraint. It asks legislators to recognize fallibility, judges to work within settled law, and institutions to avoid turning moral dislike into public terror. Yet Montesquieu’s own social hierarchies and acceptance of different legal arrangements for different orders limit the equality of his program. The useful distinction is between explaining how moderate monarchy historically worked and treating its estate privileges as timeless requirements. His critique of cruelty and arbitrariness can travel farther than his favored institutions, but that extension is later interpretation rather than a position he fully completed.',
  ]},
  {id: 'climate', title: 'Climate, geography, and human agency', paragraphs: [
    'Books XIV–XVIII connect climate, terrain, fertility, and modes of subsistence with bodies, habits, laws, and political possibilities. Montesquieu proposed physiological mechanisms by which heat and cold shaped sensation and character, many of which are scientifically obsolete. He nevertheless did not treat physical influence as an irresistible fate. Laws could accommodate conditions or counteract damaging tendencies, and moral causes such as religion, commerce, education, and government interacted with physical ones. The relevant historical distinction is between causal influence and absolute determinism, not between modern science and complete irrationality.',
    'Even qualified climate theory carries serious risks. Generalizations about vigorous northern peoples and passive southern peoples helped organize Eurocentric political geography and could naturalize domination. Montesquieu sometimes uses climate to explain why slavery appears more “natural” or tolerable in hot countries after declaring slavery bad in itself. That tension cannot be solved by citing his anti-slavery satire alone. Readers should identify the explanatory ambition, reject the superseded physiology and stereotypes, and track how environmental arguments can shift responsibility away from institutions and enslavers onto imagined traits of populations.',
  ]},
  {id: 'commerce', title: 'Commerce, manners, and political economy', paragraphs: [
    'Montesquieu argues that commerce can soften manners by making people encounter difference, calculate mutual advantage, and prefer exchange to conquest. Commercial interdependence may support peace and weaken certain prejudices. But commerce is not an automatic school of virtue. It can make every human service a matter of price, encourage narrow interest, and coexist with luxury, monopoly, colonial coercion, or fiscal power. His analysis is comparative: commerce operates differently under republics, monarchies, and despotisms, and its political effects depend on laws, credit, taxation, property, and international rivalry.',
    'The so-called doux commerce thesis should therefore be treated as one tension in a larger political economy. Montesquieu studies bills of exchange, ports, conquest, public debt, population, and the historical paths by which European states acquired commercial practices. The merchant’s interest may check martial glory, yet imperial commerce can enlist state force. This ambivalence influenced later Scottish Enlightenment accounts, including Smith’s, while leaving open whether commercial society moderates rulers or creates new concentrations of power. Montesquieu offers no simple prophecy that markets civilize every relation.',
  ]},
  {id: 'slavery', title: 'Slavery: condemnation and compromise', paragraphs: [
    'The Spirit of the Laws begins its treatment of civil slavery by calling the institution bad for both enslaved person and master. Montesquieu rejects conquest, voluntary sale, inherited status, and racist or religious rationalizations. His notorious ironic chapter on the enslavement of Africans mimics indefensible arguments so that their cruelty and absurdity become visible. Persian Letters had already linked the seraglio’s power to slavery and shown domination from the subject’s perspective. These passages became resources for later anti-slavery argument.',
    'Yet Montesquieu does not sustain an unqualified abolitionist conclusion. He considers slavery more tolerable under despotism and partly reconcilable with reason in extremely hot climates, and he discusses regulations within slave systems. Climate thus reintroduces conditional justification after natural-law defenses have been dismantled. Scholars dispute how irony, prudence, and contextual explanation affect the verdict, but the mixed record must remain explicit. Calling him simply pro-slavery ignores the critique; calling him simply an abolitionist erases concessions that could and did support environmental rationalizations of bondage.',
  ]},
  {id: 'empire-romans', title: 'Rome, conquest, and empire', paragraphs: [
    'Considerations on the Romans asks how disciplined institutions, adaptable military practices, conflict among orders, and incorporation of conquered peoples helped Rome expand—and how expansion transformed the republic that produced it. Success changes scale, wealth, command, and civic motive. Armies become attached to generals, distant provinces feed corruption, and institutions suited to a city cannot govern a vast empire without alteration. Rome is not simply an ancient model to imitate; it demonstrates how causes of greatness can become causes of decline.',
    'The Spirit of the Laws extends this analysis to conquest and international order. Montesquieu opposes universal monarchy and values a plurality of states whose powers can balance, while also examining federations and the law of nations. His European balance is historically situated and does not remove imperial hierarchy from the commercial world he describes. Persian satire, Roman history, and comparative law each register domination beyond the metropole, but not through a modern anti-colonial framework. His work is strongest when showing that conquest deforms conquerors’ institutions as well as conquered peoples’ lives.',
  ]},
  {id: 'reception-reading', title: 'Moderate enlightenment and a careful reading path', paragraphs: [
    'Montesquieu shaped constitutional thought, comparative law, historical sociology, penal reform, political economy, and the federal arguments of the American founders. Later readers often extracted separation of powers from the relational analysis that gave it meaning. Others made him a founder of liberalism or sociology, labels that can orient reception but should not be projected as his own disciplinary identity. His defense of intermediary bodies, legal security, religious toleration, and restrained punishment supports a politics of moderation, while his climate generalizations, estate assumptions, and compromised slavery analysis mark serious limits.',
    'Begin with Persian Letters, especially the early Paris observations and final seraglio sequence, to learn how voice and irony work. Read Considerations on the Romans next as a compact exercise in historical causation. Then approach The Spirit of the Laws through Books I–V on law and government, XI–XII on liberty and punishment, XIV–XVII on climate and slavery, and XX–XXI on commerce. The Cambridge Companion helps preserve disputes over empire, slavery, women, economy, and sovereignty. The goal is not a three-branch slogan but a disciplined habit of asking which relations make law moderate or despotic.',
  ]},
];

const smithArticle: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'overview', title: 'Moral judgment and commercial society', paragraphs: [
    'Adam Smith wrote one project across moral philosophy, jurisprudence, rhetoric, history, and political economy, even though only two major books appeared in his lifetime. The Theory of Moral Sentiments asks how embodied, partial people become capable of judging themselves and others. The Wealth of Nations asks how labor, exchange, law, policy, and power shape collective opulence. Neither book says that selfishness is the only human motive. Sympathy, resentment, ambition, prudence, vanity, justice, bargaining, public spirit, and the desire for approval operate in different settings and must be understood together.',
    'Smith is equally ill served by the label of pure laissez-faire. He attacks monopoly, mercantilist privilege, colonial companies, and official systems that direct ordinary activity for favored interests. Yet exchange depends on justice and public institutions, and government has duties involving defense, courts, infrastructure, education, taxation, and other works private profit will not reliably provide. Commercial society can widen independence and improve material life while deforming workers’ capacities and corrupting admiration. Smith’s analysis is therefore institutional and moral, not a celebration of markets detached from law and history.',
  ]},
  {id: 'life-context', title: 'Kirkcaldy, Glasgow, and the Scottish Enlightenment', paragraphs: [
    'Born in Kirkcaldy in 1723, Smith studied at Glasgow under Francis Hutcheson and later at Balliol College, Oxford. He returned to Scotland to lecture in Edinburgh, then held chairs of logic and moral philosophy at Glasgow. His teaching ranged across ethics, jurisprudence, government, and political economy. The Theory of Moral Sentiments appeared in 1759 and was repeatedly revised through a sixth edition in 1790. Smith’s friendships and arguments linked him with David Hume and a wider Scottish Enlightenment network while his formation also drew on Stoicism, natural jurisprudence, moral-sense theory, and historical inquiry.',
    'Smith left Glasgow to tutor the young Duke of Buccleuch in Europe, meeting French thinkers including the Physiocrats. He then spent years composing The Wealth of Nations, published in 1776. Later service as a commissioner of customs is sometimes presented as ironic, but it also placed him inside the fiscal and commercial institutions he analyzed. Student notes from his lectures on jurisprudence survive; they are valuable but not an authorized finished treatise. Smith ordered many manuscripts destroyed before his death in 1790, so reconstructions of a complete unwritten system require caution.',
  ]},
  {id: 'sympathy', title: 'Sympathy as imaginative fellow-feeling', paragraphs: [
    'In The Theory of Moral Sentiments, sympathy means an imaginative fellow-feeling with another person’s situation, not simply benevolence or emotional contagion. Because we do not directly feel another’s experience, we construct what we might feel in like circumstances. The spectator tries to enter the agent’s case, while the agent moderates expression so spectators can accompany it. Moral judgment grows from this search for correspondence between perspectives. Sympathy can concern grief, anger, joy, gratitude, or resentment; it is a mechanism of social understanding, not proof that people are naturally selfless.',
    'The process is imperfect. Vivid proximity draws attention, social rank distorts imagination, and spectators may fail to grasp unfamiliar suffering. Agents learn self-command partly because other people cannot match the intensity of their passions. Smith’s theory therefore explains both moral connection and systematic bias. General rules develop from repeated judgments, but rules do not replace perceptive attention to situations. The account begins within ordinary social life rather than from an external moral formula, leaving later readers to debate how sentiment can criticize entrenched convention when spectators themselves share its exclusions.',
  ]},
  {id: 'impartial-spectator', title: 'The impartial spectator and self-command', paragraphs: [
    'By imaginatively dividing oneself into agent and observer, a person forms the standpoint Smith calls the impartial spectator. This is not a literal public poll and not an infallible inner voice. It is a disciplined effort to view one’s conduct from a fairer distance, informed by encounters with actual spectators but capable of correcting their applause. Smith distinguishes the desire to be praised from the desire to be genuinely praiseworthy. Conscience becomes socially formed without being reducible to whatever a surrounding group rewards.',
    'The impartial spectator supports self-command, justice, beneficence, and prudence in different ways. It can rebuke vanity and the worship of wealth, yet its authority develops through historically situated practices. Smith’s later revisions increasingly stress the “man within the breast” against a deceived external audience. That move helps explain moral resistance but does not eliminate questions about whose experience the imagined observer understands. The model’s strength is its dynamic combination of emotion, imagination, distance, and rule; its vulnerability is that partial social materials may continue to shape the supposedly impartial view.',
  ]},
  {id: 'virtue-justice', title: 'Virtue, resentment, and the grammar of justice', paragraphs: [
    'Smith does not reduce virtue to sympathy. Prudence concerns the agent’s own flourishing; beneficence positively promotes others’ good; justice restrains injury; self-command regulates passion; humanity and generosity answer particular relations. Justice has a distinctive strictness because improper harm arouses resentment in victim and spectators. Beneficence cannot ordinarily be forced, but violations of justice may be punished. This makes justice a necessary foundation of society rather than the whole of excellence. A society can survive limited generosity; it cannot survive when members expect violence and fraud without remedy.',
    'Rules of justice are more exact than rules for virtues such as friendship or generosity, yet their content develops historically through property, status, contract, and government. Smith’s unpublished jurisprudence project and surviving lecture reports connect natural resentment with changing legal institutions. He neither supplies a timeless code deduced from self-ownership nor treats law as whatever states enact. The framework joins moral psychology to institutional history. Its unresolved question is how impartial judgment should evaluate legal orders that protect settled expectations while those expectations reflect conquest, class power, slavery, or colonial privilege.',
  ]},
  {id: 'wealth-labor', title: 'Labor, specialization, and opulence', paragraphs: [
    'The Wealth of Nations opens with division of labor because specialization can multiply productive power through dexterity, saved time, and invention. Exchange lets people coordinate specialized work without one planner assigning every task. The celebrated pin-making example is an analytic illustration, not evidence that Smith invented factories or personally discovered all gains from specialization. Markets expand the feasible division of labor, while transport, security, capital, skills, and institutions shape markets. National wealth is the annual produce available to people, not a treasury’s stock of bullion.',
    'Specialization has moral costs as well as productive benefits. Repetition can narrow a worker’s mind and weaken capacities for judgment, conversation, and civic or military participation. Smith therefore argues for public attention to basic education. He also studies wages, profits, rents, bargaining, and class interests rather than assuming exchange occurs among equals. Employers can combine more easily than workers, and policy is often influenced by merchants and manufacturers. “Labor” in Smith is embedded in unequal institutions; productivity does not by itself settle who receives its benefits or what forms of life it creates.',
  ]},
  {id: 'prices-markets', title: 'Prices, competition, and institutional order', paragraphs: [
    'Smith distinguishes market prices, which respond to current supply and demand, from natural prices that cover ordinary wages, profit, and rent under prevailing conditions. Competition can move prices and resources, but it operates through laws of property, contract, incorporation, apprenticeship, settlement, taxation, and trade. His “system of natural liberty” is an argument against particular mercantilist restrictions and privileges, not a description of a preinstitutional market. Government never vanishes from the account because justice, revenue, money, infrastructure, and security structure economic action.',
    'The famous invisible-hand phrase appears rarely and should not stand for a universal theorem that private greed always maximizes welfare. Smith’s analyses of unintended coordination sit beside examples of monopoly, collusion, external danger, informational limits, and policies captured by organized interests. Self-interest can support exchange because each party offers terms another can accept; selfishness can also seek monopoly and political favor. The proper contrast is not benevolence versus markets but different institutional channels through which mixed motives operate. Competition must be protected from the powerful actors who benefit when it is suspended.',
  ]},
  {id: 'commercial-society', title: 'Commercial society and independence', paragraphs: [
    'Smith’s conjectural histories describe changes from hunting through pastoral, agricultural, and commercial arrangements, linking subsistence with property and government. These stages are explanatory models built from eighteenth-century evidence, not current anthropology and not a single inevitable ladder followed by every people. Commercial society can loosen personal dependence on feudal superiors by letting individuals obtain goods through many exchanges. It can also create new dependence on wages, distant markets, credit, and institutions. The result is neither liberation pure and simple nor moral collapse.',
    'The Theory of Moral Sentiments supplies a psychology of commercial ambition. Admiration for wealth and greatness can stimulate industry and produce public benefits through unintended effects, yet it also corrupts moral judgment when people neglect the poor and flatter the powerful. Smith does not solve this tension by condemning material improvement. He distinguishes genuine prudence and comfort from the anxious pursuit of status. Commercial society should be judged by the condition and capacities of ordinary people, the security of justice, and resistance to privilege—not by aggregate riches alone or by an ideal of ascetic civic virtue.',
  ]},
  {id: 'empire', title: 'Mercantilism, colonies, and empire', paragraphs: [
    'Book IV attacks the mercantile system for treating national wealth as bullion and organizing policy around the interests of merchants and manufacturers. Colonial monopolies force consumers and producers into privileged channels while military and administrative costs fall on the wider public. Chartered companies can combine commercial incentives with sovereign power, producing especially destructive government. Smith’s criticism reaches conquest, monopoly, and the fiscal burden of empire, but he writes within Britain’s imperial world and does not offer a modern theory of decolonization.',
    'On the American colonies Smith considers alternatives including separation and an imperial federation with representation and taxation. This makes his position more complex than either champion of empire or consistent anti-imperialist. He condemns monopoly and the blood and treasure spent for favored interests, while imagining reformed political union rather than rejecting all imperial association. His discussions of enslaved labor criticize its inefficiency and the domination of masters, but economic and moral objections do not amount to a fully developed abolitionist politics. Those boundaries should remain visible beside the power of his critique.',
  ]},
  {id: 'state-functions', title: 'Justice, public works, education, and revenue', paragraphs: [
    'Smith assigns the sovereign duties of defense, administration of justice, and public works or institutions whose social benefit cannot repay private investors. His examples include roads, bridges, canals, coinage, and education, with varied proposals for fees, local administration, and public contribution. He analyzes taxation through equality, certainty, convenience, and economy. These are not a blueprint for a contemporary welfare state, but they decisively contradict the image of a thinker who thought government should only protect contracts and then disappear.',
    'Government action itself needs institutional scrutiny. Military establishments, universities, churches, corporations, regulators, and revenue officers can become complacent or captured. Smith often uses incentives and divided authority to make public institutions answerable to their purposes. Yet public provision is needed where private return and common benefit diverge, and education is a remedy for injuries produced by the division of labor. His position is not “intervention whenever beneficial” or “intervention never”: it is a comparative inquiry into who bears costs, who has information and power, and what arrangements sustain justice and public capacity.',
  ]},
  {id: 'unity-tensions', title: 'One Smith, without forced harmony', paragraphs: [
    'The nineteenth-century “Adam Smith problem” alleged a contradiction between sympathy in The Theory of Moral Sentiments and self-interest in The Wealth of Nations. The contrast rests on misreading both terms. Sympathy is a process of perspective-taking, not universal benevolence; self-interest is one motive especially relevant to exchange, not the whole of human psychology. Smith revised Moral Sentiments after Wealth of Nations without renouncing it. His books address different questions within a connected account of social judgment, historical institutions, and unintended order.',
    'Connection does not require perfect harmony. Smith’s confidence in ordinary judgment sits beside awareness that rank corrupts spectators. Commercial independence sits beside wage dependence and cognitive damage. Natural liberty sits beside historically specific property and imperial power. His admiration for Stoic self-command can strain against attention to vulnerability. These are fruitful tensions rather than proof of two unrelated authors. A careful interpretation moves between the moral agent, legal order, class structure, and political state instead of selecting the passage that best fits a current ideology.',
  ]},
  {id: 'reception-reading', title: 'From moral philosophy to modern economics', paragraphs: [
    'Smith became a foundational figure in political economy and later economics, but modern disciplinary categories can hide the range of his inquiry. Free-market advocates, social critics, imperial reformers, and historians of moral philosophy have each claimed different Smiths. The phrase “father of capitalism” is retrospective: capitalism was not his settled name for one system, and his work contains neither a defense of selfishness nor a timeless equilibrium model. His influence travels through contested interpretations of labor, value, trade, growth, institutions, sentiment, and state capacity.',
    'Begin with The Theory of Moral Sentiments, Part I on sympathy and Part III on conscience, then read its discussions of rank, virtue, and moral systems. In The Wealth of Nations, read Books I–II on labor and accumulation, Book IV on mercantilism and colonies, and Book V on public expense and education. Use the surviving jurisprudence lectures as mediated student reports. Specialist companions help join the works without erasing revision or context. The guiding question is how ordinary people can become materially secure and morally capable within institutions that channel partial knowledge and unequal power.',
  ]},
];

const wollstonecraftArticle: ArticleSection[] = [
  {id: 'overview', title: 'Rights require independent virtue', paragraphs: [
    'Mary Wollstonecraft’s argument for women’s rights begins from moral equality: if human beings are capable of reason and accountable for virtue, institutions must cultivate that capacity rather than train one sex for dependence. Rights are not merely permissions to pursue preference. They secure the independence needed for judgment, work, friendship, citizenship, marriage, and responsible care. Education is therefore political, because artificial weakness becomes evidence for the hierarchy that produced it. Men are also corrupted when rank, luxury, and arbitrary authority teach them to value domination over character.',
    'Her philosophy emerged through many genres: educational advice, novels, translations, hundreds of reviews, revolutionary pamphlets, history, travel writing, and unfinished fiction. A Vindication of the Rights of Woman is central but not self-sufficient. Rights of Men attacks hereditary privilege; the Scandinavian Letters study commerce, feeling, nature, and family through travel; Maria dramatizes marriage law and class from women’s constrained positions. “Feminist” usefully names her reception and challenge to sex hierarchy, but historically responsible reading also locates her in eighteenth-century languages of virtue, providence, civilization, sensibility, and revolution.',
  ]},
  {id: 'life-literary-work', title: 'Making a life through education and print', paragraphs: [
    'Born in London in 1759 into a family whose resources declined, Wollstonecraft received less formal education than her brother and moved repeatedly with her father’s unsuccessful ventures. She worked in occupations available to middling women: companion, schoolteacher, and governess. With her sisters and Fanny Blood she attempted to run a school at Newington Green, where contact with the dissenting minister Richard Price connected personal educational experience with reformist religious and political networks. Fanny’s death after childbirth in Lisbon and the school’s collapse shaped her early writing without mechanically explaining its arguments.',
    'Publisher Joseph Johnson enabled Wollstonecraft to become a professional writer, translator, reviewer, and editorial contributor in London. Her work for the Analytical Review exposed her to education, theology, travel, science, fiction, aesthetics, and current politics. She traveled to revolutionary France, formed a relationship with Gilbert Imlay, and had a daughter, Fanny. After their separation and two suicide attempts, she journeyed to Scandinavia on business for Imlay. She later married William Godwin and died in 1797 from complications after giving birth to Mary, later Mary Shelley. Biography matters, but should not eclipse the authored corpus.',
  ]},
  {id: 'rights-men-revolution', title: 'Rights of Men and the Revolution controversy', paragraphs: [
    'A Vindication of the Rights of Men answered Edmund Burke’s Reflections during the British debate over the French Revolution. Wollstonecraft attacks hereditary rank, primogeniture, accumulated property, courtly dependence, and an aesthetic politics that invites sympathy for titled sufferers while obscuring ordinary deprivation. Virtue cannot pass through bloodlines, and institutions deserve allegiance because they support justice, not because age makes them sacred. Her language combines natural rights, dissenting moral religion, republican independence, and criticism of commercial luxury rather than repeating one later liberal or socialist platform.',
    'The pamphlet also exposes gender in political rhetoric. Burke’s theatrical tenderness, chivalry, and idealized femininity make dependence appear beautiful. Wollstonecraft counters that feeling severed from reason can protect hierarchy. Yet she does not reject emotion; she seeks affections educated toward truth and general benevolence. Her early enthusiasm for the French Revolution was later tested by violence and political exclusion, especially in An Historical and Moral View. The shift is critical judgment within revolutionary commitment, not a simple conversion from radicalism to conservatism.',
  ]},
  {id: 'rights-woman', title: 'Rights of Woman: one moral standard', paragraphs: [
    'A Vindication of the Rights of Woman addresses women as members of the human species before assigning them a decorative sexual character. If virtue depends on reason and self-government, women must receive the education and civil standing needed to practice it. Wollstonecraft targets conduct books, elite manners, military hierarchy, aristocratic idleness, and writers such as Rousseau who make pleasing men the organizing purpose of female formation. Apparent frivolity, cunning, and emotional excess are predictable adaptations to powerlessness, not proof of a fixed female nature.',
    'Her equality claim is moral and developmental rather than a denial of every bodily difference. She sometimes concedes uncertain differences in physical strength while refusing to let them establish intellectual or civic subordination. The work proposes national coeducation, useful learning, exercise, professions, and a public role for mothers as educators of citizens. Some institutional proposals remain schematic, and political rights such as women’s representation are gestured toward more briefly than education. The argument’s force lies in linking personal character to the legal and economic conditions that make independence possible.',
  ]},
  {id: 'education', title: 'Education as civic reconstruction', paragraphs: [
    'Wollstonecraft’s educational thought begins before the Vindications in Thoughts on the Education of Daughters and Original Stories. She opposes accomplishments designed only for marriage markets and learning based on obedience without understanding. Girls and boys need bodily exercise, intellectual challenge, moral habits, practical competence, and occasions to judge consequences. Coeducation can make future relations less theatrical by allowing the sexes to know one another as fellow learners. Public day schools also keep children connected to family and social diversity rather than isolating them in elite boarding institutions.',
    'Education cannot compensate for every structural dependence. A woman trained to reason but barred from occupations, property, political voice, or escape from an abusive marriage remains vulnerable. Conversely, rights without cultivated judgment may reproduce vanity and rank. Wollstonecraft joins both sides: institutions should expand independence, and education should form people capable of using it virtuously. Her confidence in disciplined reason belongs to the Enlightenment, while her pedagogy values affection and imagination when ordered toward truth. It is neither rote rationalism nor a theory that spontaneous feeling automatically liberates.',
  ]},
  {id: 'virtue-dependence', title: 'Virtue, reason, and dependence', paragraphs: [
    'Virtue for Wollstonecraft requires acting from understood principles rather than fear, seduction, or the wish to please a superior. Dependence encourages duplicity because people denied direct power learn to maneuver indirectly. This diagnosis applies to women, courtiers, soldiers, servants, and others formed under arbitrary rank. Independence does not mean isolation or refusal of care. It means standing as a moral agent capable of reciprocal attachment, work, and judgment. Friendship is a favored model because it seeks esteem among persons rather than worship across hierarchy.',
    'Her language of masculine virtue can jar modern readers. Often she uses “masculine” polemically for strength that society falsely reserves to men, inviting women to claim common human qualities. At other points she reproduces gendered or classed judgments about softness, fashion, servants, and popular culture. Historical context explains the rhetorical field but does not erase hierarchy within her own reform. A careful reading distinguishes the universal challenge to sex subordination from particular eighteenth-century standards of respectable independence through which she sometimes articulates it.',
  ]},
  {id: 'marriage-family', title: 'Marriage, friendship, and the family', paragraphs: [
    'Wollstonecraft criticizes marriage when law, education, and economic necessity make it a property relation. A wife trained to secure male protection through beauty cannot be an equal companion, and a husband accustomed to obedience cannot learn friendship. Sexual fascination is temporary; a durable household should rest on respect, shared judgment, parental responsibility, and material security. She does not reject marriage or family as such. She tries to transform them from schools of mastery into relations among citizens, while insisting that private despotism damages public virtue.',
    'Maria; or, The Wrongs of Woman makes the legal stakes visceral. Its protagonist is confined by a husband and separated from her child; Jemima’s history connects gendered violence with poverty, service, sexual exploitation, and criminalization. Because the novel was unfinished and published posthumously, no final resolution should be supplied on Wollstonecraft’s behalf. The surviving narrative broadens Rights of Woman beyond an abstract educated heroine: dependence varies by class, and legal marriage can coordinate property, sexuality, medicine, and confinement against women’s agency.',
  ]},
  {id: 'class-labor', title: 'Class, labor, and commercial modernity', paragraphs: [
    'Wollstonecraft attacks aristocratic privilege and idle luxury because both detach status from useful activity and teach society to admire appearance. She praises a broad middle condition associated with work, domestic responsibility, and comparatively independent judgment. That preference can criticize great wealth and inherited rank, but it can also marginalize servants and poor people whose dependence she interprets through a middling ideal. Her writings do not offer a complete class politics. They repeatedly ask, however, how property and occupations distribute the practical ability to become virtuous.',
    'The Scandinavian Letters complicate any simple hostility to commerce. Wollstonecraft observes ports, land, labor, fishery, trade, manners, and poverty while conducting Imlay’s business. Exchange and improvement can widen activity, yet acquisitiveness can reduce persons and landscapes to instruments. Packham’s political-economic reading helps show that her critique of women’s dependence belongs inside a larger account of commercial civilization. The traveler’s judgments remain situated, sometimes hasty, and shaped by British assumptions; their value lies partly in the visible movement between observation, feeling, economic inquiry, and self-correction.',
  ]},
  {id: 'empire-civilization', title: 'Empire, civilization, and historical limits', paragraphs: [
    'Wollstonecraft’s universal rights language creates resources for criticizing conquest and arbitrary rule. Rights of Men links hierarchy at home with domination abroad, and her travel and historical writing questions war, luxury, exploitation, and commercial power. Yet she also uses eighteenth-century contrasts among civilization, barbarism, refinement, and progress that can rank peoples through European measures. Her critical universalism is therefore not identical with a later anti-imperial or postcolonial theory. Both the reach of her equality claim and the limits of her historical vocabulary require attention.',
    'Her analogies among women, enslaved people, soldiers, and subjects of tyranny expose common mechanisms of dependence, but analogy can also obscure distinct institutions and experiences. She opposed slavery and domination without producing a sustained analysis of Atlantic plantation economies comparable to her account of women’s education. Readers should not manufacture one. The stronger approach maps where empire, trade, travel, race, and national character enter the corpus, then asks how her principle—that no rational being should be formed for another’s arbitrary use—presses beyond the cases she most fully developed.',
  ]},
  {id: 'literary-context', title: 'Reviews, fiction, travel, and sensibility', paragraphs: [
    'Wollstonecraft’s philosophy was made in a literary marketplace. Her Analytical Review work assessed novels, sermons, travel accounts, pedagogy, natural history, and aesthetics, developing a public critical voice through engagement with other writers. She worried that formulaic sentimental fiction encouraged vanity and passive identification, yet her own prose uses feeling, scene, and first-person address. The issue is educated sensibility: emotions should disclose suffering and connection without becoming theatrical substitutes for judgment. This tension joins her literary criticism to her attack on Burke and conduct-book femininity.',
    'Mary, A Fiction and Maria explore how women’s desire and imagination are formed under narrow options. The Scandinavian Letters combine travel report, political economy, natural description, maternal reflection, and personal grief, helping reshape Romantic prose. Generic variety is philosophical method rather than an ornamental supplement to the Vindications. Fiction can show dependency from within; reviews test standards across culture; travel writing puts judgment in motion. None should be mined as a transparent diary, and the published Letters should not be confused with raw private correspondence.',
  ]},
  {id: 'tensions-reception', title: 'Tensions and contested inheritances', paragraphs: [
    'Wollstonecraft’s work contains tensions between universal human virtue and gendered rhetoric, equal education and special maternal duties, independence and moral discipline, democratic criticism of rank and preference for middling respectability, cosmopolitan rights and civilizational hierarchy. These are not reasons to collapse her into her period or to project a complete contemporary feminism backward. They show a thinker using inherited concepts against institutions that those concepts had often protected, sometimes transforming them and sometimes retaining their exclusions.',
    'William Godwin’s candid Memoirs after her death helped hostile readers make her personal life a warning against women’s emancipation. Her reception was never total silence: her works circulated, were translated, and influenced rights advocates across countries, though later nineteenth-century debate often avoided her name. Twentieth-century feminist recovery sometimes celebrated her as a solitary first modern feminist, a genealogy that can hide predecessors, contemporaries, religious commitments, and global reception. She is better understood as a major Enlightenment moral and political philosopher whose afterlives were plural and contested.',
  ]},
  {id: 'reading-path', title: 'A historically situated reading path', paragraphs: [
    'Begin with A Vindication of the Rights of Men to see Wollstonecraft attack hereditary power, property, sentimentality, and Burke before turning to sex equality. Then read Rights of Woman straight through rather than excerpting only its criticism of Rousseau: education, virtue, marriage, class, citizenship, and national reform form one argument. Compare chapters on female manners with the institutional school proposal so that the diagnosis of character remains linked to its social causes. SEP and the Cambridge Companion provide the historical vocabulary needed to avoid anachronistic shortcuts.',
    'Follow with the Scandinavian Letters, attending to commerce, labor, landscape, motherhood, feeling, and the limits of the traveler’s gaze. Read Maria last and remember its unfinished, posthumous status; Jemima’s narrative expands the class and legal range of the political works. Selected Analytical Review essays illuminate how criticism, aesthetics, and philosophy developed together. The most useful question is not whether Wollstonecraft matches a present category perfectly, but how rights, independence, education, affection, work, and institutions combine in her effort to make virtue possible for women and men.',
  ]},
];

